## Why This Is Asked
Design a scalable, auditable CDC ingestion that evolves schemas safely while preserving privacy and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Central schema registry with versioned contracts
- Idempotent upserts and MERGE semantics
- Dynamic schema evolution and tenant isolation
- Late data buffering and privacy-preserving BI views
- End-to-end testing and monitoring of contracts and lineage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: contract validation and upsert
function upsertEvent(event, registry) {
  const contract = registry.getContract(event.version, event.schemaKey);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  // perform idempotent merge into Iceberg
  mergeIntoIceberg(event, contract.primaryKey);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift in production and implement safe rollback?
- How would you monitor per-tenant data isolation and lineage end-to-end?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly construction of a data pipeline with common tasks: deduplication, validation, timestamp handling, and partitioned Parquet, plus testing and monitoring.

## Key Concepts
- JSON Lines parsing
- Idempotent deduplication
- Data quality checks (required fields, types)
- Timestamp normalization to UTC
- Parquet writing and date partitioning
- Basic testing and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const readline = require('readline');
(async () => {
  const rl = readline.createInterface({ input: fs.createReadStream('logs.jsonl'), crlfDelay: Infinity });
  const seen = new Set();
  for await (const line of rl) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(line);
    if (!obj.user_id || !obj.timestamp || !obj.path || !obj.status) continue;
    const key = `${obj.timestamp}|${obj.user_id}|${obj.path}`;
    if (seen.has(key)) continue;
    seen.add(key);
    // normalization and persistence would occur here
  }
})();
```

